    "done_when": "From your worktree at its base revision (which carries the per-worker thread cap, the registered slow marker and the preprocess test fix), submit one SLURM batch job with sbatch on --partition=sun --time=03:00:00 --cpus-per-task=24 --mem=96G that runs, with export TMPDIR=/tmp and unset -f uv, UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/home/ITER/mcintos/Code/imas-ambix/.venv PYTHONPATH=$PWD command uv run --no-sync pytest -p no:cacheprovider -n 8 --durations=60 -q tests, the whole suite, into one log under your run directory with a header line (revision, hostname, nproc, load) and a final EXIT line. A run longer than your turn must not be a child of yours, which is why it is a batch job. Write your manifest first, record the job id, and declare the wait as wait_probe [\"sacct\",\"-n\",\"-X\",\"-P\",\"-j\",\"<job id>\",\"-o\",\"State\"] with wait_terminal [\"COMPLETED\",\"FAILED\",\"TIMEOUT\",\"CANCELLED\",\"OUT_OF_MEMORY\",\"NODE_FAIL\"], then park. When resumed, the evidence fragment docs/evidence/fragments/project-environment-declared/ped-timeout-suite-final.html records the pass, fail, error and skip counts, the wall time, and every pytest-timeout abort with its test id and phase, classified against the tables of ped-timeout-latent.html, ped-timeout-worldmodel.html, ped-timeout-corpus.html and ped-timeout-preprocess.html as accounted for or not (an ERROR at setup counts by the test id it names); it lists every non-timeout failure or error with its first error line as pre-existing, without fixing it, and states the collection error count. Status complete when no abort is unaccounted for and no collection error remains; blocked otherwise, naming the tests. Nothing outside the fragment changes.",
